From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: domicile CI <ci@domicile.invalid>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 16:39:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] domicile: let gn gen accept ozone_platform_drm off ChromeOS

ui/ozone/platform/drm/BUILD.gn opens with assert(is_chromeos), and
//ui/ozone/BUILD.gn makes platform/drm:gbm a dependency of //ui/ozone the
moment ozone_platform_drm is true, so gn gen refuses before anything compiles.
That assert is the whole of what stood between this fork and a tty, and it is
conservative about the platform it guards: at this pin the 49 .cc files in
//ui/ozone/platform/drm:gbm contain two references to ChromeOS between them and
zero BUILDFLAG(IS_CHROMEOS). Both are incidental, and this moves each under an
is_chromeos of its own rather than deleting it.

page_flip_watchdog.cc asked ash::switches::IsRevenBranding() for a page-flip
flake threshold and emitted Platform.FlexPageFlipFlakes2. Flex is a ChromeOS
brand: off ChromeOS the threshold is the ordinary constant and the histogram is
not recorded at all, because a Flex experiment fed by builds that are not Flex
is worse than one with no data from them.

ozone_platform_drm.cc returned ash::InputMethodAsh from CreateInputMethod. Off
ChromeOS it returns InputMethodMinimal, which is exactly what
ozone_platform_headless.cc already returns on Linux, out of //ui/base/ime,
which this target already depends on.

Those two account for the whole of the target's //ash/constants and
//ui/base/ime/ash deps -- the only entries in its dependency list that carry an
is_chromeos assert of their own -- so those two lines move under if
(is_chromeos) and nothing else in the deps list changes. Everything else it
names builds on desktop Linux at this pin: //build/config/linux/libdrm asserts
(is_linux || is_chromeos), //third_party/minigbm resolves to a pkg_config on
system Mesa when use_system_minigbm, //ui/events/ozone/evdev asserts
(is_linux || is_chromeos) with every ChromeOS dep inside an if, and
//ui/display/types has no assert at all.

AND THEN A COMPILER FOUND WHAT READING THE SOURCE COULD NOT. With the five
edits above, gn gen accepted `ozone_platform_drm = true` and autoninja got 9336
of 9413 steps into //ui/ozone before stopping on this:

  ../../ui/ozone/platform/drm/gpu/hardware_display_plane_manager_atomic.cc:345:34:
    error: no member named 'kCtmColorManagement' in namespace 'display::features'

`display::features::kCtmColorManagement` and
`display::features::kDrmColorSpaceDefaultIsRec709` are declared inside a
`#if BUILDFLAG(IS_CHROMEOS)` in ui/display/display_features.h. The namespace is
there on Linux and the members are not, which is exactly the shape of thing a
`git grep` for ChromeOS in this platform does not see: no ash, no chromeos/, no
BUILDFLAG in the file, just a name that exists in one configuration. There are
four such call sites -- three for CTM colour management across
hardware_display_plane_manager{,_atomic}.cc and one for the Rec709 default in
drm_display.cc -- and each now takes the branch a disabled flag would take, in
a `#if BUILDFLAG(IS_CHROMEOS)` beside the call. No flag is invented: a
`base::Feature` that upstream declares only for ChromeOS is not something a
fork should conjure a Linux twin of, and "off" is a behaviour upstream already
ships and tests on the same code path.

`PlaneToOutputMatrix` goes under the same guard, because its only caller is one
of those four and an unused function in an anonymous namespace is an error
under Chromium's -Werror.

AND THEN THE COMPILER FOUND SOMETHING ELSE. With those in place the build got
to 9373 of 9412 and stopped on:

  ../../ui/ozone/platform/drm/gpu/drm_thread_proxy.cc:42:12:
    error: use of undeclared identifier 'ERROR'
       42 |       PLOG(ERROR) << "Unable to initialize GBM for " << path.value();

drm_thread_proxy.cc uses PLOG and does not include base/logging.h. It compiles
on ChromeOS because something on that configuration's include path drags the
header in; off it, nothing does. That is an ordinary include-what-you-use gap
upstream has never had to notice, and the fix is to include what is used.

Seven files in this platform use LOG, PLOG, VLOG or SYSLOG without including
base/logging.h -- found by asking for the whole class at once rather than one
fifteen-minute build at a time, because a probe that finds these one per run is
a probe nobody will finish running. drm_thread_proxy.cc is the one that was
measured; the other six are the same query's answer. drm_util.cc also uses
NOTREACHED() without base/notreached.h, which base/logging.h does not include at
this pin, so it gets both. Each include is correct on ChromeOS too -- the header
is already there, transitively -- so none of this changes that build.

AND ONE MORE, WHICH ONLY A LINKER COULD HAVE SAID. With all of the above the
platform's 49 .cc files all compiled and `libui_ozone.so` failed to link:

  mold: error: undefined symbol: ui::CursorController::GetInstance()
  >>> referenced by drm_cursor.cc

`ui/events/ozone/BUILD.gn:44` compiles `chromeos/cursor_controller.{cc,h}` only
`if (is_chromeos)`. The header is in the tree on every platform, so the include
resolves, the call compiles, and the symbol is simply never defined -- a shape
no compile-time check and no `git grep` was going to catch. It is also the one
the audit's own search missed, because it looked for `include "chromeos/` and
the path here is `ui/events/ozone/chromeos/`. `CursorController` applies the
per-window cursor transform ChromeOS needs for display rotation and
magnification; off ChromeOS there is no such configuration to apply, so the
delta is used as it arrived.

GATED RATHER THAN DROPPED, for gbm_unittests. //ui/ozone/BUILD.gn appends
platform/drm:gbm_unittests to ozone_platform_test_deps unconditionally when the
argument is true, so dropping the target moves the gn gen failure instead of
removing it. Three of its sources go under is_chromeos instead:
drm_display_unittest.cc and screen_manager_unittest.cc reach for
ui/display/manager/test/fake_display_snapshot.h, which //ui/display:test_support
compiles only if (is_chromeos) because //ui/display/manager asserts it; and
hardware_display_plane_manager_unittest.cc drives kCtmColorManagement directly.
The other nineteen still build. Unlike everything above, that gating is reasoned
rather than measured -- the probe builds //ui/ozone, which does not include this
target.

+ash/constants/ash_switches.h leaves gpu/DEPS. That file is read by checkdeps,
not by gn, so it has no effect on any build this fork configures -- and
checkdeps does not evaluate the preprocessor, so on an is_chromeos build it
would now flag the include that remains under the guard above. That is
upstream's presubmit on a configuration this fork never builds, and the
alternative -- a DEPS rule for a header no Domicile build compiles -- is the
worse of the two.

THE ASSERT IS RELAXED, NOT REMOVED, and the difference is the point. This
platform's CreateScreen() and InitScreen() are NOTREACHED(), because on ChromeOS
ash installs display::Screen itself; and the only caller of
NativeDisplayDelegate::Configure in the tree is display::DisplayConfigurator,
whose target opens with assert(is_chromeos). So a build with this patch and
nothing else comes up with the DRM devices open, the snapshots read, and every
CRTC still off. is_linux here says the build can be configured, not that a
screen lights up; the embedder that lights one is step 2 of
docs/architecture/A-DESKTOP-ON-A-TTY.md.

THIS IS A NO-OP FOR THE SHIPPED ENGINE, and that is checked rather than assumed.
scripts/build.sh and .github/scripts/engine-release-build.sh both configure with
ozone_auto_platforms = false and name only ozone_platform_wayland and
ozone_platform_headless; build/config/ozone.gni defaults ozone_platform_drm to
false and only turns it on under ozone_auto_platforms with is_chromeos. With the
argument false, //ui/ozone/BUILD.gn never adds platform/drm:gbm to
ozone_platform_deps, so ui/ozone/platform/drm/BUILD.gn is not loaded, none of
the five .cc files is compiled, and gpu/DEPS is not read by anything in the
build. Every line this patch touches is inside a file that the shipped
configuration does not reach -- and the engine workflow, which runs every guard
against a tree with this series applied, is what asserts that rather than this
paragraph.
